<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Faraday</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Faraday was an English scientist who discovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electromagnetic-induction">electromagnetic induction</a> in 1831, the principle behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-generator">electric generators</a> and transformers. Largely self-educated, he worked at the Royal Institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he also made major discoveries in electrochemistry, built an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-motor">electric motor</a> and gave popular lectures on science.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-faraday.jpg" alt="Michael Faraday" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael_Faraday_sitting_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Science and thought flourished: Charles Darwin’s On the Origin of Species (1859), the work of <b>Michael Faraday</b> and James Clerk Maxwell on electromagnetism, and the economics of David Ricardo and John Stuart Mill.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Michael Faraday</b> first demonstrated electromagnetic induction on 29 August 1831, and Joseph Henry discovered it independently in 1832.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-invented-it-first">Who Invented It First?</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Newington Butts, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Hampton Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.49,-0.1&amp;year=1840" aria-label="Newington Butts on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4548 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Michael Faraday"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4998 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4998 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.49,-0.1&amp;year=1840">What was here in 1840? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is no better, there is no more open door by which you can enter into the study of natural philosophy, than by considering the physical phenomena of a candle.&#8221;</p><footer>The Chemical History of a Candle (1861), lecture 1 &#183; original &#183; Christmas lectures for young people at the Royal Institution &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14474/pg14474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…that you may, like it, shine as lights to those about you; that, in all your actions, you may justify the beauty of the taper by making your deeds honourable and effectual in the discharge of your duty to your fellow-men.&#8221;</p><footer>The Chemical History of a Candle (1861), closing words &#183; original &#183; Christmas lectures for young people at the Royal Institution &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14474/pg14474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
